# SRG-APP-000028-AU-000600  -  CAT II
# The Central Log Server must automatically audit account disabling actions.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000028-AU-000600
group:           V-221902
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
cci:             CCI-001404
control_rev5:    AC-2

contribution:    Disabling a Splunk-native account is itself an account modification that
                 splunkd records in its audit trail without a configurable switch; the
                 application's configurable share is that those audit events are queued to
                 an index so the disabling action is retained and searchable.

governs:         audit.conf - The audit trail Splunk keeps of its own activity, and how those records
                 are signed.

check:           audit.conf [auditTrail] queueing not_equals false

external:        Splunk Enterprise has no account disable state of its own for identities
                 held in an external store; disabling an LDAP or SAML identity happens in
                 the directory service or identity provider, which generates that record
                 and may forward it to Splunk as event data.
external:        Where audit queueing is false by design, an inputs.conf monitor stanza
                 tailing the audit log carries retention of these records.
external:        The organizational procedure that decides when an account is disabled is
                 outside the application.

citation:        splunk-spec-files/audit.conf.spec:23-29 ([auditTrail] queueing =
                 <boolean>, default true); audit.conf declares no other setting in 10.x;
                 SRG-APP-000028-AU-000600 check and fix text.
finding:         Audit records for account disabling actions are generated by Splunk but
                 not retained in an index, because audit.conf [auditTrail] queueing is set
                 to false and no compensating monitor input is configured; this is assessed
                 at the application layer alone, and disabling of identities held in an
                 external directory service or identity provider is audited by that
                 component, which is recorded as an external dependency.
